Step 1 - Understand the problem
Write a JavaScript function that fetches JSON from a URL using async/await with error handling.

Step 2 - Plan the solution
1. Mark the function async so await can be used.
2. await fetch(url) to get the response.
3. Check res.ok, because fetch does not throw on 404 or 500.
4. Parse with await res.json() and wrap everything in try/catch.

Step 3 - Write the code
async function getData(url) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Fetch failed:', err);
    return null;
  }
}

Step 4 - Test it and check edge cases
Test with a valid URL, a 404 URL and with the network turned off.

Note: Always handle errors in async code.

