Step 1 - Understand the problem
Write a Java method that implements bubble sort on an int array.

Step 2 - Plan the solution
1. The outer loop controls the number of passes.
2. The inner loop compares neighbours and swaps them if out of order.
3. Each pass places the largest remaining value at the end, so the inner range shrinks.
4. If a pass makes no swaps, stop early.

Step 3 - Write the code
public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
        boolean swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < arr.length - 1 - i; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                int tmp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = tmp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) break;
    }
}

Step 4 - Test it and check edge cases
{5, 1, 4, 2} -> {1, 2, 4, 5}. Already sorted input finishes in one pass.

Note: Time O(n^2), best case O(n). Fine for learning, use Arrays.sort in real code.

